{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Vbwa3cVgbgu37ELr1GHG7i","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Integrated design and copy acceptance\n2\t\n3\tLoad at section planning, the Step 4/5 grayscale review and the Step 8 pre-handoff check. This is the rulebook for `tools/pre-handoff-check.mjs`, not an optional separate review. The machine thresholds are in `runtime/quality-checks.json`; semantic verdicts and evidence live in the same `handoff.json` used by the checker. A missing required manual check fails acceptance. Automated detectors identify measurable patterns; a human/independent critic checks meaning and visual exceptions. Neither is an authorship detector. Impeccable findings are advisory: its `pass` means the pinned detector ran and produced parseable output, not that it found no design problems. The gate separately requires the scoped critic/manual PASS records for generic heroes, rounded-card grids, dashboard tropes, decoration and other semantic criteria; absent or failed records block handoff.\n4\t\n5\t## Measurement contract\n6\t\n7\tThe reference viewport is 1440 × 900 CSS pixels at device scale one, with a full-page screenshot as well. Visit every declared screen and applicable state. Wait for local fonts and assets; log failed or blocked loads. Keep external requests, service workers and WebSockets blocked during the local check. Capture file hashes and the version under test. A reference PNG is 1440 pixels wide and named `screen--state--locale--vNN.png`; its hash identifies the version. A screenshot from another revision cannot support a PASS. The checker also renders every declared `settings.desktopWidths` width and 320 CSS px (the 400% reflow equivalent of a 1280 px desktop) and fails horizontal overflow, text that actually overflows a hidden or clipped box (ellipsis styling alone is not a failure; `body` is screened too, so `overflow-x: hidden` or `clip` on `html` and `body` cannot hide a wide layout), text or media pushed past the left edge (except inside a horizontal scroller, visually hidden sr-only content of at most 1×1 px or `clip: rect(0 0 0 0)` / `clip-path: inset(50%)`, and a focusable off-screen skip link that appears on focus) and a language/direction mismatch; this is a screen, not mobile approval, and manual zoom and text-spacing evidence stays required.\n8\t\n9\tMark visible text wrappers with `data-copy-slot=\"ID\"` and match every displayed string to the approved locale variant in the copy deck, including interface and error labels. Unlisted or mismatched text blocks final handoff. Declare semantic section, component, copy-slot and media IDs in the reference DOM and match them to the handoff inventory. The critic inspects the full screenshot and semantic outline so unmarked containers cannot evade a count. CSS/computed-style counts are recorded as measurements. Layout intent, claims, tasteful restraint and the purpose of an image remain reviewed judgments with an artifact, reviewer, explanation and brief line references. A guessed PASS flag is not evidence.\n10\t\n11\tWhere the browser test uses a proxy, label it as a proxy in `evidence.json`. In particular, DOM element rectangles do not measure the actual colored pixels of text, images, gradients or overlapping regions. For accent coverage, the documented acceptance measure is visible accent area divided by total rendered area, separately for the full page and each reviewed viewport. The reviewer supplies an annotated color mask or measured screenshot regions for image/canvas/SVG content and any disputed DOM estimate. Use union area, not summed overlapping boxes. Borderline or unmeasurable coverage remains FAIL pending evidence; do not round 10.1% down to 10%. The automated accent screen fails closed: if a declared accent token (`--accent`, `--colors-accent`, `--colors-primary`) is not a valid colour, or no accent token is declared, the ratios are null and the handoff check fails; it is never treated as zero accent.\n12\t\n13\tThe automated probes and axe run in a separate browser world (`tools/probe-world.mjs`, a CDP isolated world; Chromium only): they see the page's DOM and CSS but not its JavaScript globals, so a page script cannot patch `getComputedStyle`, `querySelectorAll` or `axe` to hide a failure. `tools/integrity.mjs` cross-checks the positional page: the same probes in the page's own world (a difference is a tampering sign), the page world's probe functions (they must still be native), and a JavaScript-off pass. More glass or a failing 320 px screen with JavaScript off fails as `integrity mismatch` unless the handoff's `jsOffDifferences` records the expected difference with a reason; contrast is not cross-checked with JavaScript off.\n14\t\n15\tAt the 320 px reflow width, ordinary text or media overflowing any sideways scroller, to the left or the right, fails. A `table`, `pre`, `code`, `role=\"grid\"`/`role=\"table\"` with real rows, or an element marked `data-fannan-2d=\"reason\"` is only a **candidate**, recorded in `evidence.json` with its element and reason and never excused automatically: its scroller must fit inside 320 px and be keyboard-usable, the text around it must reflow, resizable media is not a candidate, and every candidate needs a `reflowExceptions` decision in the handoff, or the final handoff is blocked. There is no cap on the number of candidates.\n16\t\n17\tThe accent must be the approved palette token (`colors.accent`, else `colors.primary`, from the hash-checked tokens). At final handoff an invalid or missing accent variable, or one that differs from the approved token, fails with a message naming the variable; the wireframe phase records the accent as DEFERRED, never PASS. The accent area is a DOM background-area proxy and is approximate; the screenshot review decides. The automated check does not measure accent area or coverage exactly and does not fail other off-palette colours painted on the page: accent area, coverage and stray colours are decided by the screenshots and the manual `accent-area` and `palette` reviews (panel A; R12 M19 closed on that basis). The accent does not have to appear on a control.\n18\t\n19\tThe spacing-scale probe checks all four margins (logical margins included; `auto` and 0 are ignored), padding and gaps. An element marked `data-fannan-spacing-exception=\"reason\"` is recorded with its element, id, reason, values and screen/state/lang in the handoff evidence (`checks.spacingExceptions`). At final handoff it excuses its values only when the handoff has a matching `spacingExceptions` entry for that element id and screen/state/lang, with a reason and the approver as reviewer; otherwise the handoff is blocked (wireframes record it as DEFERRED). A blank reason, or an element without an id, excuses nothing. Margins on `html` and `body` are screened too.\n20\t\n21\tNegative spacing **tokens** are banned: the approved scale holds only positive lengths, so a negative value is always off-scale. An approved margin exception (above, with the approver named) may still excuse a negative margin on one element; that is a reviewed per-element decision, not a token (R13 D1 closed on this basis).\n22\t\n23\tFrames are never excused: there is no `frameExceptions` entry. axe and the design probes run in the main document only, so any `iframe`, `frame`, `object`, `embed` or `fencedframe` fails the final handoff (DEFERRED in wireframes). Where the design needs embedded third-party content (a map, a video, a booking widget), the handoff uses a **static poster** image in its place plus an **engineering note** in `HANDOFF.md` naming the embed, its source and where the engineer inserts it. In-frame scanning is revisited only once every gate runs inside frames (R13 q1).\n24\t\n25\t## Pass/fail checklist\n26\t\n27\tEvery row is BLOCK unless explicitly marked WARN. `wireframe` evaluates the structure/copy column; visual-only criteria are recorded as deferred, never called PASS. `handoff` requires both columns and all fourteen manual IDs listed below.\n28\t\n29\t| Rule | Pass mark | Evidence and detector | Phase |\n30\t|---|---|---|---|\n31\t| Point of view | One coherent idea tied to an audience, job and goal; all three cited | `point-of-view`: brief lines, rationale and annotated recommended direction | Both |\n32\t| Section and decision trace | 100% of sections, layout breaks, token choices and expressive media name the brief line and visitor job served | `brief-trace`: section/decision ledger matched to DOM IDs and screenshot | Both; token trace after styling |\n33\t| Centering | At most one centered principal section per page | DOM computed text alignment plus composition screenshot; reviewer catches nested/unmarked groups | Both |\n34\t| Accent area | At most 10% full-page and at most 10% reviewed viewport | Declared accent token(s), computed-style proxy, screenshot measurement including accent inside media/effects | Visual |\n35\t| Type sizes | At most six distinct rendered sizes per page/locale, all mapped to named type tokens | Token list and DOM computed `font-size`; inspect pseudo-elements/SVG/canvas text manually | Visual; structural hierarchy in grayscale |\n36\t| Centered icon trio | Zero rows of three centered icon-first feature blocks | DOM grouping and screenshot; changing tags or using SVG does not exempt it | Both |\n37\t| Generic hero | Zero vague transferable headlines, including a vague headline above three icon cards | `generic-hero`: writer/critic swap test and actual hero screenshot; a headline still valid for unrelated firms fails | Both |\n38\t| Default effects | Zero generic gradient heroes and zero stock glassmorphism | Background-image/backdrop-filter detector (computed on `html`, `body`, visible elements and rendered `::before`/`::after`, inside open shadow roots, plus constructed stylesheets and the raw text of every served CSS/HTML/SVG/JS file, style attributes and `<style>` elements, so the `-webkit-` alias and root-element filters that Chrome drops still fail; `data:`/`blob:` stylesheets are refused and unreadable unserved ones fail; same-origin frames are only partially inspected, for glass and width, and every `iframe`, `frame`, `object`, `embed` or `fencedframe` fails the final handoff) plus screenshot; decorative effects cannot be relabelled data visuals | Both in grayscale; visual final |\n39\t| Rounded-card grid | Zero repeated default rounded-card grids | `card-grid`: actual grouped content, borders/radii and screenshot; use the content's appropriate structure | Both |\n40\t| Purple-blue default | Zero default purple-blue decorative pairing | Color-role inventory and screenshot; semantic status color is reviewed for its job, never used as a second decorative accent | Visual |\n41\t| Decoration | Every non-content mark has a named comprehension, navigation, hierarchy or identity job | `decoration-job`: asset/element ID → brief line → purpose; unsupported marks are removed | Both |\n42\t| Dashboard tropes | Zero undifferentiated KPI-card rows, icon soup, color-only status, fake live tickers | `dashboard-tropes`: task-flow review, screenshots, data/source/timestamp labels and state checks | Both |\n43\t| Copy provenance | Every slot's displayed text appears in the brief or writer's deck; every claim maps to a checked, confirmed fact | Slot source lines + source revision/hash; writer checks names/location/partners against project rules | Both |\n44\t| Copy filler | Zero occurrences of `unlock`, `seamless`, `empower`, `cutting-edge` and their listed variants in visible copy | English word-boundary and normalized Arabic cliché scan; critic read-through in every required language | Both |\n45\t| Rule-of-three tagline | Zero empty three-part slogans | Headline-only automated punctuation/conjunction screening plus mandatory `rule-of-three` semantic review; useful three-item task/data lists are not slogans | Both |\n46\t| Stats/quotes/logos | Zero unconfirmed or unlicensed proof claims | Fact ledger, exact quotation/source, permission and asset manifest; numeric regex is only a warning detector for this broader review | Both |\n47\t| Placeholders | All visible as `[placeholder]` with IDs during draft; zero at final handoff | Copy deck + rendered DOM + placeholder inventory; scan lorem ipsum, TBD and hidden substitutes | Draft recorded; final BLOCK |\n48\t| Craft | Consistent named type/spacing/grid/color roles and purposeful hierarchy | `craft`: token sheet, alignment overlay, actual content and comparative reference critique | Structural before style; complete at handoff |\n49\t| Real reference comparison | Three inspected references with dated notes and project-specific lessons | `reference-work`: source URLs, captures/notes, what to adopt and reject, brief trace | Both |\n50\t| Complete accessibility evidence | No failed applicable WCAG 2.2 A/AA criterion or unresolved automated incomplete result | `wcag-review`: axe (tags `wcag2a`, `wcag2aa`, `wcag21a`, `wcag21aa`, `wcag22a`, `wcag22aa` from `tools/axe-tags.mjs`, plus `target-size`; axe's experimental A/AA rules stay off and are manual) + keyboard/screen-reader/manual checklist + reference-state captures | Both at design level; full proof at handoff |\n51\t| All states/locales and copy fit | Every requested variant accounted for; no clipping, truncation or meaningless N/A | Component matrix, real translations, 150% synthetic stress evidence, LTR/RTL review | Both at current fidelity |\n52\t| Observational improvement | Optional non-blocking preference with owner and disposition | WARN only when no contract, accessibility, truth, approval or implementation question is affected | Both |\n53\t\n54\tSix sizes serve six semantic tiers without making every label another style. Ten percent makes “about ten percent” a reproducible upper bound. The rules are intentionally strict constraints requested for this bot; they do not establish universal rules of good design.\n55\t\n56\t## Manual evidence that the checker requires\n57\t\n58\t`manualChecks` includes `point-of-view`, `brief-trace`, `generic-hero`, `decoration-job`, `card-grid`, `dashboard-tropes`, `rule-of-three`, `reference-work`, `craft`, `wcag-review`, `accent-area`, `palette`, `rules-copy` and `language-overflow`. Every record identifies its screen, language and state, PASS/FAIL, reviewer, artifact path, brief line references and a concrete note. An artifact may cover several states, but each record identifies the part supporting its exact scope. The artifact contains the actual rubric/check result, screenshot references and measured values where relevant. Missing coverage for a screen is a failure even if another screen passed. A grayscale artifact records visual tests as deferred while judging structural craft; it does not sign off final color or typography.\n59\t\n60\tThe critic asks: with the company name masked, does the headline still express a precise offering, audience task or independently sourced distinction from this brief? Record the compared alternative categories and the reason. If it could be pasted unchanged onto arbitrary company sites, fail and return the slot to the writer. Do not replace an approved headline without the existing approval process.\n61\t\n62\tFor functional dashboards, derive content from the user's decisions and workflows. Use a ranked queue, trend or table when that is what the task requires; do not add a summary-card row merely because dashboards often have one. Every number requires a real source, scope and update meaning. Static sample data must be explicitly labelled as a fixture and cannot enter a real handoff as production data. “Live” requires a real documented data path; animation is not evidence.\n63\t\n64\t## Accessibility evidence, accurately scoped\n65\t\n66\tRecord each text foreground/background pair and state. Normal text needs 4.5:1 contrast; large text needs 3:1 using WCAG's definition of large text. Contrast uses specified/computed colors, not antialiased screenshot edges. Do not round a failing ratio upward. [W3C contrast guidance](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/contrast-minimum.html).\n67\t\n68\tApplicable control boundaries, meaningful icons and graphical objects need 3:1 against adjacent colors. Record their actual role and applicable exceptions rather than claiming every decorative border must pass. Status also has a readable label or shape. [W3C non-text contrast](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/non-text-contrast.html).\n69\t\n70\tRecord sequential keyboard focus, visible focus before/after, modal entry/escape/return, traps and sticky overlays. AA focus-not-obscured requires that the focused control is not entirely hidden by author content; prefer full visibility as this project's stricter usability target. Focus Appearance 2.4.13 is AAA, so do not mislabel it as the AA criterion. [W3C focus-not-obscured](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/focus-not-obscured-minimum).\n71\t\n72\tMeasure actual pointer targets. A 24 × 24 CSS-pixel target is the baseline; any smaller target needs a documented qualifying spacing, equivalent-control, inline, user-agent or essential exception. Bounding boxes and an “inline” tag are only screening heuristics. [W3C target-size guidance](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/target-size-minimum).\n73\t\n74\tTest non-dragging alternatives for dragging operations, and accessible authentication without an unsupported cognitive test where authentication exists. If absent, record N/A with the screen inventory that proves absence. [W3C WCAG 2.2 additions](https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/new-in-22/).\n75\t\n76\tCheck 200% text resizing and 400% desktop zoom/reflow (equivalent 320 CSS-pixel width), including applicable two-dimensional content exceptions. This is accessibility validation, not authorization to design phone layouts. Document browser zoom and viewport used. [W3C reflow guidance](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/reflow.html).\n77\t\n78\tSet the page language, mark language changes and specify base direction separately. Test real RTL content, mixed-direction identifiers, punctuation and numbers; use appropriate directional markup rather than reversing strings. [W3C internationalization guidance](https://www.w3.org/International/questions/qa-html-dir).\n79\t\n80\t## Command and release decision\n81\t\n82\t```sh\n83\tFANNAN_DEPS=/opt/fannan/deps \\\n84\tnode \"$FANNAN_RELEASE/tools/pre-handoff-check.mjs\" \\\n85\t  /workspace/design-output/PROJECT/DESIGN.md \\\n86\t  /workspace/design-output/PROJECT/reference.html \\\n87\t  /workspace/design-output/PROJECT/evidence-vNN \\\n88\t  PROJECT /workspace/design-output/PROJECT/handoff.json --phase=handoff\n89\t```\n90\t\n91\tUse `--phase=wireframe` for the Step 4/5 structural check. Replace `PROJECT` with the validated project slug and use a fresh output directory. A nonzero result blocks. A missing protected checker binary, dependency, reference, approval or manual evidence remains a failure; never replace it with a stub in a production run. The separate test suite may stub a boundary only when its output is clearly labelled test-only and cannot masquerade as release evidence.\n92\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8f80f814-38ed-4ed2-80fc-18e1bfbe6740","uuid":"bc62fd73-b79e-40d3-8095-6f932e69c4b4","timestamp":"2026-10-18T03:25:45.115Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/panel-r14/fannan/release/references/design-quality.md","content":"# Integrated design and copy acceptance\n\nLoad at section planning, the Step 4/5 grayscale review and the Step 8 pre-handoff check. This is the rulebook for `tools/pre-handoff-check.mjs`, not an optional separate review. The machine thresholds are in `runtime/quality-checks.json`; semantic verdicts and evidence live in the same `handoff.json` used by the checker. A missing required manual check fails acceptance. Automated detectors identify measurable patterns; a human/independent critic checks meaning and visual exceptions. Neither is an authorship detector. Impeccable findings are advisory: its `pass` means the pinned detector ran and produced parseable output, not that it found no design problems. The gate separately requires the scoped critic/manual PASS records for generic heroes, rounded-card grids, dashboard tropes, decoration and other semantic criteria; absent or failed records block handoff.\n\n## Measurement contract\n\nThe reference viewport is 1440 × 900 CSS pixels at device scale one, with a full-page screenshot as well. Visit every declared screen and applicable state. Wait for local fonts and assets; log failed or blocked loads. Keep external requests, service workers and WebSockets blocked during the local check. Capture file hashes and the version under test. A reference PNG is 1440 pixels wide and named `screen--state--locale--vNN.png`; its hash identifies the version. A screenshot from another revision cannot support a PASS. The checker also renders every declared `settings.desktopWidths` width and 320 CSS px (the 400% reflow equivalent of a 1280 px desktop) and fails horizontal overflow, text that actually overflows a hidden or clipped box (ellipsis styling alone is not a failure; `body` is screened too, so `overflow-x: hidden` or `clip` on `html` and `body` cannot hide a wide layout), text or media pushed past the left edge (except inside a horizontal scroller, visually hidden sr-only content of at most 1×1 px or `clip: rect(0 0 0 0)` / `clip-path: inset(50%)`, and a focusable off-screen skip link that appears on focus) and a language/direction mismatch; this is a screen, not mobile approval, and manual zoom and text-spacing evidence stays required.\n\nMark visible text wrappers with `data-copy-slot=\"ID\"` and match every displayed string to the approved locale variant in the copy deck, including interface and error labels. Unlisted or mismatched text blocks final handoff. Declare semantic section, component, copy-slot and media IDs in the reference DOM and match them to the handoff inventory. The critic inspects the full screenshot and semantic outline so unmarked containers cannot evade a count. CSS/computed-style counts are recorded as measurements. Layout intent, claims, tasteful restraint and the purpose of an image remain reviewed judgments with an artifact, reviewer, explanation and brief line references. A guessed PASS flag is not evidence.\n\nWhere the browser test uses a proxy, label it as a proxy in `evidence.json`. In particular, DOM element rectangles do not measure the actual colored pixels of text, images, gradients or overlapping regions. For accent coverage, the documented acceptance measure is visible accent area divided by total rendered area, separately for the full page and each reviewed viewport. The reviewer supplies an annotated color mask or measured screenshot regions for image/canvas/SVG content and any disputed DOM estimate. Use union area, not summed overlapping boxes. Borderline or unmeasurable coverage remains FAIL pending evidence; do not round 10.1% down to 10%. The automated accent screen fails closed: if a declared accent token (`--accent`, `--colors-accent`, `--colors-primary`) is not a valid colour, or no accent token is declared, the ratios are null and the handoff check fails; it is never treated as zero accent.\n\nThe automated probes and axe run in a separate browser world (`tools/probe-world.mjs`, a CDP isolated world; Chromium only): they see the page's DOM and CSS but not its JavaScript globals, so a page script cannot patch `getComputedStyle`, `querySelectorAll` or `axe` to hide a failure. `tools/integrity.mjs` cross-checks the positional page: the same probes in the page's own world (a difference is a tampering sign), the page world's probe functions (they must still be native), and a JavaScript-off pass. More glass or a failing 320 px screen with JavaScript off fails as `integrity mismatch` unless the handoff's `jsOffDifferences` records the expected difference with a reason; contrast is not cross-checked with JavaScript off.\n\nAt the 320 px reflow width, ordinary text or media overflowing any sideways scroller, to the left or the right, fails. A `table`, `pre`, `code`, `role=\"grid\"`/`role=\"table\"` with real rows, or an element marked `data-fannan-2d=\"reason\"` is only a **candidate**, recorded in `evidence.json` with its element and reason and never excused automatically: its scroller must fit inside 320 px and be keyboard-usable, the text around it must reflow, resizable media is not a candidate, and every candidate needs a `reflowExceptions` decision in the handoff, or the final handoff is blocked. There is no cap on the number of candidates.\n\nThe accent must be the approved palette token (`colors.accent`, else `colors.primary`, from the hash-checked tokens). At final handoff an invalid or missing accent variable, or one that differs from the approved token, fails with a message naming the variable; the wireframe phase records the accent as DEFERRED, never PASS. The accent area is a DOM background-area proxy and is approximate; the screenshot review decides. The automated check does not measure accent area or coverage exactly and does not fail other off-palette colours painted on the page: accent area, coverage and stray colours are decided by the screenshots and the manual `accent-area` and `palette` reviews (panel A; R12 M19 closed on that basis). The accent does not have to appear on a control.\n\nThe spacing-scale probe checks all four margins (logical margins included; `auto` and 0 are ignored), padding and gaps. An element marked `data-fannan-spacing-exception=\"reason\"` is recorded with its element, id, reason, values and screen/state/lang in the handoff evidence (`checks.spacingExceptions`). At final handoff it excuses its values only when the handoff has a matching `spacingExceptions` entry for that element id and screen/state/lang, with a reason and the approver as reviewer; otherwise the handoff is blocked (wireframes record it as DEFERRED). A blank reason, or an element without an id, excuses nothing. Margins on `html` and `body` are screened too.\n\nNegative spacing **tokens** are banned: the approved scale holds only positive lengths, so a negative value is always off-scale. An approved margin exception (above, with the approver named) may still excuse a negative margin on one element; that is a reviewed per-element decision, not a token (R13 D1 closed on this basis).\n\nFrames are never excused: there is no `frameExceptions` entry. axe and the design probes run in the main document only, so any `iframe`, `frame`, `object`, `embed` or `fencedframe` fails the final handoff (DEFERRED in wireframes). Where the design needs embedded third-party content (a map, a video, a booking widget), the handoff uses a **static poster** image in its place plus an **engineering note** in `HANDOFF.md` naming the embed, its source and where the engineer inserts it. In-frame scanning is revisited only once every gate runs inside frames (R13 q1).\n\n## Pass/fail checklist\n\nEvery row is BLOCK unless explicitly marked WARN. `wireframe` evaluates the structure/copy column; visual-only criteria are recorded as deferred, never called PASS. `handoff` requires both columns and all fourteen manual IDs listed below.\n\n| Rule | Pass mark | Evidence and detector | Phase |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Point of view | One coherent idea tied to an audience, job and goal; all three cited | `point-of-view`: brief lines, rationale and annotated recommended direction | Both |\n| Section and decision trace | 100% of sections, layout breaks, token choices and expressive media name the brief line and visitor job served | `brief-trace`: section/decision ledger matched to DOM IDs and screenshot | Both; token trace after styling |\n| Centering | At most one centered principal section per page | DOM computed text alignment plus composition screenshot; reviewer catches nested/unmarked groups | Both |\n| Accent area | At most 10% full-page and at most 10% reviewed viewport | Declared accent token(s), computed-style proxy, screenshot measurement including accent inside media/effects | Visual |\n| Type sizes | At most six distinct rendered sizes per page/locale, all mapped to named type tokens | Token list and DOM computed `font-size`; inspect pseudo-elements/SVG/canvas text manually | Visual; structural hierarchy in grayscale |\n| Centered icon trio | Zero rows of three centered icon-first feature blocks | DOM grouping and screenshot; changing tags or using SVG does not exempt it | Both |\n| Generic hero | Zero vague transferable headlines, including a vague headline above three icon cards | `generic-hero`: writer/critic swap test and actual hero screenshot; a headline still valid for unrelated firms fails | Both |\n| Default effects | Zero generic gradient heroes and zero stock glassmorphism | Background-image/backdrop-filter detector (computed on `html`, `body`, visible elements and rendered `::before`/`::after`, inside open shadow roots, plus constructed stylesheets and the raw text of every served CSS/HTML/SVG/JS file, style attributes and `<style>` elements, so the `-webkit-` alias and root-element filters that Chrome drops still fail; `data:`/`blob:` stylesheets are refused and unreadable unserved ones fail; same-origin frames are only partially inspected, for glass and width, and every `iframe`, `frame`, `object`, `embed` or `fencedframe` fails the final handoff) plus screenshot; decorative effects cannot be relabelled data visuals | Both in grayscale; visual final |\n| Rounded-card grid | Zero repeated default rounded-card grids | `card-grid`: actual grouped content, borders/radii and screenshot; use the content's appropriate structure | Both |\n| Purple-blue default | Zero default purple-blue decorative pairing | Color-role inventory and screenshot; semantic status color is reviewed for its job, never used as a second decorative accent | Visual |\n| Decoration | Every non-content mark has a named comprehension, navigation, hierarchy or identity job | `decoration-job`: asset/element ID → brief line → purpose; unsupported marks are removed | Both |\n| Dashboard tropes | Zero undifferentiated KPI-card rows, icon soup, color-only status, fake live tickers | `dashboard-tropes`: task-flow review, screenshots, data/source/timestamp labels and state checks | Both |\n| Copy provenance | Every slot's displayed text appears in the brief or writer's deck; every claim maps to a checked, confirmed fact | Slot source lines + source revision/hash; writer checks names/location/partners against project rules | Both |\n| Copy filler | Zero occurrences of `unlock`, `seamless`, `empower`, `cutting-edge` and their listed variants in visible copy | English word-boundary and normalized Arabic cliché scan; critic read-through in every required language | Both |\n| Rule-of-three tagline | Zero empty three-part slogans | Headline-only automated punctuation/conjunction screening plus mandatory `rule-of-three` semantic review; useful three-item task/data lists are not slogans | Both |\n| Stats/quotes/logos | Zero unconfirmed or unlicensed proof claims | Fact ledger, exact quotation/source, permission and asset manifest; numeric regex is only a warning detector for this broader review | Both |\n| Placeholders | All visible as `[placeholder]` with IDs during draft; zero at final handoff | Copy deck + rendered DOM + placeholder inventory; scan lorem ipsum, TBD and hidden substitutes | Draft recorded; final BLOCK |\n| Craft | Consistent named type/spacing/grid/color roles and purposeful hierarchy | `craft`: token sheet, alignment overlay, actual content and comparative reference critique | Structural before style; complete at handoff |\n| Real reference comparison | Three inspected references with dated notes and project-specific lessons | `reference-work`: source URLs, captures/notes, what to adopt and reject, brief trace | Both |\n| Complete accessibility evidence | No failed applicable WCAG 2.2 A/AA criterion or unresolved automated incomplete result | `wcag-review`: axe (tags `wcag2a`, `wcag2aa`, `wcag21a`, `wcag21aa`, `wcag22a`, `wcag22aa` from `tools/axe-tags.mjs`, plus `target-size`; axe's experimental A/AA rules stay off and are manual) + keyboard/screen-reader/manual checklist + reference-state captures | Both at design level; full proof at handoff |\n| All states/locales and copy fit | Every requested variant accounted for; no clipping, truncation or meaningless N/A | Component matrix, real translations, 150% synthetic stress evidence, LTR/RTL review | Both at current fidelity |\n| Observational improvement | Optional non-blocking preference with owner and disposition | WARN only when no contract, accessibility, truth, approval or implementation question is affected | Both |\n\nSix sizes serve six semantic tiers without making every label another style. Ten percent makes “about ten percent” a reproducible upper bound. The rules are intentionally strict constraints requested for this bot; they do not establish universal rules of good design.\n\n## Manual evidence that the checker requires\n\n`manualChecks` includes `point-of-view`, `brief-trace`, `generic-hero`, `decoration-job`, `card-grid`, `dashboard-tropes`, `rule-of-three`, `reference-work`, `craft`, `wcag-review`, `accent-area`, `palette`, `rules-copy` and `language-overflow`. Every record identifies its screen, language and state, PASS/FAIL, reviewer, artifact path, brief line references and a concrete note. An artifact may cover several states, but each record identifies the part supporting its exact scope. The artifact contains the actual rubric/check result, screenshot references and measured values where relevant. Missing coverage for a screen is a failure even if another screen passed. A grayscale artifact records visual tests as deferred while judging structural craft; it does not sign off final color or typography.\n\nThe critic asks: with the company name masked, does the headline still express a precise offering, audience task or independently sourced distinction from this brief? Record the compared alternative categories and the reason. If it could be pasted unchanged onto arbitrary company sites, fail and return the slot to the writer. Do not replace an approved headline without the existing approval process.\n\nFor functional dashboards, derive content from the user's decisions and workflows. Use a ranked queue, trend or table when that is what the task requires; do not add a summary-card row merely because dashboards often have one. Every number requires a real source, scope and update meaning. Static sample data must be explicitly labelled as a fixture and cannot enter a real handoff as production data. “Live” requires a real documented data path; animation is not evidence.\n\n## Accessibility evidence, accurately scoped\n\nRecord each text foreground/background pair and state. Normal text needs 4.5:1 contrast; large text needs 3:1 using WCAG's definition of large text. Contrast uses specified/computed colors, not antialiased screenshot edges. Do not round a failing ratio upward. [W3C contrast guidance](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/contrast-minimum.html).\n\nApplicable control boundaries, meaningful icons and graphical objects need 3:1 against adjacent colors. Record their actual role and applicable exceptions rather than claiming every decorative border must pass. Status also has a readable label or shape. [W3C non-text contrast](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/non-text-contrast.html).\n\nRecord sequential keyboard focus, visible focus before/after, modal entry/escape/return, traps and sticky overlays. AA focus-not-obscured requires that the focused control is not entirely hidden by author content; prefer full visibility as this project's stricter usability target. Focus Appearance 2.4.13 is AAA, so do not mislabel it as the AA criterion. [W3C focus-not-obscured](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/focus-not-obscured-minimum).\n\nMeasure actual pointer targets. A 24 × 24 CSS-pixel target is the baseline; any smaller target needs a documented qualifying spacing, equivalent-control, inline, user-agent or essential exception. Bounding boxes and an “inline” tag are only screening heuristics. [W3C target-size guidance](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/target-size-minimum).\n\nTest non-dragging alternatives for dragging operations, and accessible authentication without an unsupported cognitive test where authentication exists. If absent, record N/A with the screen inventory that proves absence. [W3C WCAG 2.2 additions](https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/new-in-22/).\n\nCheck 200% text resizing and 400% desktop zoom/reflow (equivalent 320 CSS-pixel width), including applicable two-dimensional content exceptions. This is accessibility validation, not authorization to design phone layouts. Document browser zoom and viewport used. [W3C reflow guidance](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/reflow.html).\n\nSet the page language, mark language changes and specify base direction separately. Test real RTL content, mixed-direction identifiers, punctuation and numbers; use appropriate directional markup rather than reversing strings. [W3C internationalization guidance](https://www.w3.org/International/questions/qa-html-dir).\n\n## Command and release decision\n\n```sh\nFANNAN_DEPS=/opt/fannan/deps \\\nnode \"$FANNAN_RELEASE/tools/pre-handoff-check.mjs\" \\\n  /workspace/design-output/PROJECT/DESIGN.md \\\n  /workspace/design-output/PROJECT/reference.html \\\n  /workspace/design-output/PROJECT/evidence-vNN \\\n  PROJECT /workspace/design-output/PROJECT/handoff.json --phase=handoff\n```\n\nUse `--phase=wireframe` for the Step 4/5 structural check. Replace `PROJECT` with the validated project slug and use a fresh output directory. A nonzero result blocks. A missing protected checker binary, dependency, reference, approval or manual evidence remains a failure; never replace it with a stub in a production run. The separate test suite may stub a boundary only when its output is clearly labelled test-only and cannot masquerade as release evidence.\n","numLines":92,"startLine":1,"totalLines":92}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_bdrk_01Vbwa3cVgbgu37ELr1GHG7i","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"content":[{"id":"toolu_bdrk_01UXJjDxLqoRLNCweKkNCjdZ","input":{"file_path":"/workspace/panel-r14/fannan/release/references/critic-rubric.md"},"name":"Read","type":"tool_use"}],"id":"msg_e4ecff3a87154ace8181db417e0ad43e","model":"claude-opus-5-5","role":"assistant","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":17590,"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8f80f814-38ed-4ed2-80fc-18e1bfbe6740","uuid":"a9fa185d-f34f-469d-beab-5a6ccf8165b5","timestamp":"2026-10-18T03:25:45.144Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01UXJjDxLqoRLNCweKkNCjdZ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Critic rubric — copy and design together\n2\t\n3\tThe critic is independent and read-only. Load the frozen brief, rules, settings, copy/fact ledgers, reference board, scoped screen/state renders and integrated quality evidence. Do not start from the designer's self-assessment. Evaluate each page and all scoped states/locales against their real reference work; check a composition both at full-page scale and at readable 1440-pixel size. Missing evidence is FAIL, not benefit of the doubt.\n4\t\n5\t## Score anchors and pass rule\n6\t\n7\tUse zero-to-four scores only to make iteration visible: **0** absent or contradicted; **1** recognizable intent with major defects; **2** functional but generic or materially incomplete; **3** clear, specific and supported with all required checks passing; **4** unusually resolved craft, supported by concrete comparisons and detail. A score of four needs an explanation, not enthusiasm.\n8\t\n9\tEvery row and every page receives a public **PASS or FAIL**, a score, an artifact reference and a reason. PASS requires at least 3 in every applicable row, all hard checks passing and no open P0/P1. Scores are never averaged to compensate for a failed dimension. A WARN can remain only with a named owner and explicit disposition and no unanswered build question.\n10\t\n11\t| Dimension | PASS at 3 requires | Evidence / automatic failure |\n12\t|---|---|---|\n13\t| Brief and point of view | One legible idea serving this audience/job, expressed consistently across the page | Brief lines for every section and deliberate decision; any orphan section fails |\n14\t| Copy specificity and truth | Accurate approved wording, identifiable offering/task and confirmed proof | Slot/source/claim ledger, project-wording checks; transferable headline, invented proof or filler fails |\n15\t| Content hierarchy and task flow | Reading order, emphasis and CTA help the named task; first impression matches actual content | Task walkthrough and annotated frame; vague hero over icon cards or misleading primary action fails |\n16\t| Typography and language | Deliberate role pairing, at most six sizes, readable measures, correct scripts/directions and uncut translations | Token-to-DOM mapping, language renders, long-text/zoom checks; clipping or missing script support fails |\n17\t| Spacing, rhythm and grid | Repeatable spacing roles with purposeful contrast in density; any grid break aids the brief | Alignment/grid overlay and section-to-section comparison; random spacing/novelty fails |\n18\t| Color, imagery and restraint | Legible neutral base, one accent within area budget, useful licensed imagery, no default effects | Measured color roles, contrast, screenshot, asset jobs; palette/card/gradient/glass rule violation fails |\n19\t| Interaction, states and motion | Every applicable state and recovery path clear; motion explains change and has static fallback | State matrix, keyboard story, reduced-motion/no-JS proof; fake live behavior or color-only status fails |\n20\t| Accessibility and handoff precision | Applicable WCAG 2.2 AA criteria supported and each implementation choice specified | Automated plus manual evidence and canonical checklist; missing evidence, open question or unmet AA fails |\n21\t\n22\tFor a wireframe review, typography means hierarchy/readability, color means grayscale restraint, and interaction means state intent. Record color/font/motion polish as **deferred to Step 6**, outside that phase's score. Do not reward visual polish before structure is approved. The handoff rubric covers all rows without those deferrals.\n23\t\n24\t## Specificity test and reference comparison\n25\t\n26\tFor each page, quote its actual hero line from the approved copy deck, mask the brand name and compare its meaning with two unrelated business categories. If both could use it unchanged, mark `generic-hero` FAIL. A factual descriptor, named audience job or specific proposition must make the line belong to this brief. Do not invent competitor quotations. The writer owns revisions and existing approval still applies.\n27\t\n28\tCompare each page with the three inspected reference works selected in Step 3. State the exact aspect being compared, the reference evidence, where this design falls short and how the brief justifies a deliberate difference. “Looks premium” or “feels human” is not a criterion. No reference URL may be presented as inspected unless it was opened and reviewed. The current candidate contains a rubric, not a fabricated comparison against an unbuilt project.\n29\t\n30\t## Defects and the bounded loop\n31\t\n32\tP0 means a serious truth, safety, access or essential task failure; P1 means a broken required rule, missing critical evidence or a build-blocking omission. P2/P3 are lesser issues only when they do not violate a BLOCK check. A required check cannot be downgraded to make a page pass.\n33\t\n34\tAt each round record screen/state hashes, every score and PASS/FAIL, defects with severity, evidence, owner and next correction. The designer revises; the critic evaluates the new artifact independently. Stop at three rounds maximum, or earlier when total improvement is less than one point between rounds. The stop packet lists all round scores, what changed, open defects and the decision needed from the approver. It does not authorize handoff, a fourth round or relaxed thresholds. An approval cannot waive the WCAG floor, factual truth or security rules.\n35\t\n36\tThe final verdict is either **PASS for the explicitly listed scope/version** or **FAIL — return to the relevant step**. A draft, an unavailable reference or a capped loop is never “pass with conditions” for Step 9.\n37\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8f80f814-38ed-4ed2-80fc-18e1bfbe6740","uuid":"e2b152fc-929e-4949-a16b-82995a2f30c6","timestamp":"2026-10-18T03:25:45.153Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/panel-r14/fannan/release/references/critic-rubric.md","content":"# Critic rubric — copy and design together\n\nThe critic is independent and read-only. Load the frozen brief, rules, settings, copy/fact ledgers, reference board, scoped screen/state renders and integrated quality evidence. Do not start from the designer's self-assessment. Evaluate each page and all scoped states/locales against their real reference work; check a composition both at full-page scale and at readable 1440-pixel size. Missing evidence is FAIL, not benefit of the doubt.\n\n## Score anchors and pass rule\n\nUse zero-to-four scores only to make iteration visible: **0** absent or contradicted; **1** recognizable intent with major defects; **2** functional but generic or materially incomplete; **3** clear, specific and supported with all required checks passing; **4** unusually resolved craft, supported by concrete comparisons and detail. A score of four needs an explanation, not enthusiasm.\n\nEvery row and every page receives a public **PASS or FAIL**, a score, an artifact reference and a reason. PASS requires at least 3 in every applicable row, all hard checks passing and no open P0/P1. Scores are never averaged to compensate for a failed dimension. A WARN can remain only with a named owner and explicit disposition and no unanswered build question.\n\n| Dimension | PASS at 3 requires | Evidence / automatic failure |\n|---|---|---|\n| Brief and point of view | One legible idea serving this audience/job, expressed consistently across the page | Brief lines for every section and deliberate decision; any orphan section fails |\n| Copy specificity and truth | Accurate approved wording, identifiable offering/task and confirmed proof | Slot/source/claim ledger, project-wording checks; transferable headline, invented proof or filler fails |\n| Content hierarchy and task flow | Reading order, emphasis and CTA help the named task; first impression matches actual content | Task walkthrough and annotated frame; vague hero over icon cards or misleading primary action fails |\n| Typography and language | Deliberate role pairing, at most six sizes, readable measures, correct scripts/directions and uncut translations | Token-to-DOM mapping, language renders, long-text/zoom checks; clipping or missing script support fails |\n| Spacing, rhythm and grid | Repeatable spacing roles with purposeful contrast in density; any grid break aids the brief | Alignment/grid overlay and section-to-section comparison; random spacing/novelty fails |\n| Color, imagery and restraint | Legible neutral base, one accent within area budget, useful licensed imagery, no default effects | Measured color roles, contrast, screenshot, asset jobs; palette/card/gradient/glass rule violation fails |\n| Interaction, states and motion | Every applicable state and recovery path clear; motion explains change and has static fallback | State matrix, keyboard story, reduced-motion/no-JS proof; fake live behavior or color-only status fails |\n| Accessibility and handoff precision | Applicable WCAG 2.2 AA criteria supported and each implementation choice specified | Automated plus manual evidence and canonical checklist; missing evidence, open question or unmet AA fails |\n\nFor a wireframe review, typography means hierarchy/readability, color means grayscale restraint, and interaction means state intent. Record color/font/motion polish as **deferred to Step 6**, outside that phase's score. Do not reward visual polish before structure is approved. The handoff rubric covers all rows without those deferrals.\n\n## Specificity test and reference comparison\n\nFor each page, quote its actual hero line from the approved copy deck, mask the brand name and compare its meaning with two unrelated business categories. If both could use it unchanged, mark `generic-hero` FAIL. A factual descriptor, named audience job or specific proposition must make the line belong to this brief. Do not invent competitor quotations. The writer owns revisions and existing approval still applies.\n\nCompare each page with the three inspected reference works selected in Step 3. State the exact aspect being compared, the reference evidence, where this design falls short and how the brief justifies a deliberate difference. “Looks premium” or “feels human” is not a criterion. No reference URL may be presented as inspected unless it was opened and reviewed. The current candidate contains a rubric, not a fabricated comparison against an unbuilt project.\n\n## Defects and the bounded loop\n\nP0 means a serious truth, safety, access or essential task failure; P1 means a broken required rule, missing critical evidence or a build-blocking omission. P2/P3 are lesser issues only when they do not violate a BLOCK check. A required check cannot be downgraded to make a page pass.\n\nAt each round record screen/state hashes, every score and PASS/FAIL, defects with severity, evidence, owner and next correction. The designer revises; the critic evaluates the new artifact independently. Stop at three rounds maximum, or earlier when total improvement is less than one point between rounds. The stop packet lists all round scores, what changed, open defects and the decision needed from the approver. It does not authorize handoff, a fourth round or relaxed thresholds. An approval cannot waive the WCAG floor, factual truth or security rules.\n\nThe final verdict is either **PASS for the explicitly listed scope/version** or **FAIL — return to the relevant step**. A draft, an unavailable reference or a capped loop is never “pass with conditions” for Step 9.\n","numLines":37,"startLine":1,"totalLines":37}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_bdrk_01UXJjDxLqoRLNCweKkNCjdZ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
