{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_015HCFQ4as9RDCMbxZtnBH21","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Step 9 package contract\n2\t\n3\t`handoff.json` is the authored source of truth. `HANDOFF.md` is its readable checklist, generated by the checker. Keep one set of criterion IDs; do not maintain a second design/QA acceptance list. Freeze the source JSON, reference frames and all linked evidence by version/hash. Any affected change requires fresh checks and approval. Paths in the JSON are resolved from the command's working directory; use absolute paths for release evidence to avoid ambiguity.\n4\t\n5\tThis contract specifies what each real project must supply. The included synthetic fixture is only an executable checker example. It is not a real project's copy, factual proof, approval, licensed asset set or completed handoff.\n6\t\n7\t## Fixed package contents\n8\t\n9\t| Artifact | Required contents and exact acceptance |\n10\t|---|---|\n11\t| `handoff.json` and generated `HANDOFF.md` | Version 1 record; project/phase; no open questions; no placeholders; all screens, locales and states; one criterion ID per check; actual evidence and owner/reviewer |\n12\t| Source snapshot | Project settings, rules, brief, fact register and copy-deck revisions; source hashes and cited lines; current step and permitted scope |\n13\t| Private design link | `designLink.url`, `private:true` and evidence of access/privacy; tool and frame IDs/version; no public upload as a shortcut |\n14\t| `DESIGN.md` | Reviewed narrative and token frontmatter; Google format lint with zero errors; token decisions traced to brief lines |\n15\t| Tokens | DTCG-style JSON containing type, spacing, colors and breakpoints; generated CSS variables; executable build script, actual config and locked dependency versions; no hand-edited generated CSS |\n16\t| Screen/state inventory | Stable screen and component IDs; default, hover, focus, disabled, empty, loading and error all accounted for; N/A requires reason, reviewer and evidence; applicable states specify appearance, behavior and recovery |\n17\t| Reference frames | 1440-pixel-wide PNGs; `screen--state--locale--vNN.png`; actual file hash and version; every applicable state/language; reference implementation HTML when used |\n18\t| Final copy deck | Per-slot ID, screen, purpose, kind, exact final text, min/max character range, tone, language/direction, source lines, claim IDs and placement screenshot; variants use actual approved translations |\n19\t| Assets | File, source URL/document, creator, licence/permission, consent record where needed, intended use and crop; font family/weight files and licence; file size and project's budget result |\n20\t| Motion | Job, duration in ms, easing, trigger, affected element, interruption/cleanup, static reduced-motion fallback, no-JS and script-failure behavior |\n21\t| Acceptance | Per-screen criteria with ID, BLOCK/WARN, observable pass/fail check, decision and evidence. Failed BLOCK stops. WARN needs impact, owner and approved disposition with no unresolved question |\n22\t| Accessibility | WCAG 2.2 AA, contrast-pair table, focus order and captures, target sizes/exceptions, alt text and intent, heading outline, standalone action labels, manual audit and automated incomplete-result resolution |\n23\t| Audit intent | One H1 per page; complete semantic outline; intent for every image's alt text; independent link/button labels; page purpose, search intent, proposed title/description, index/canonical decision, schema type and source-backed properties or an explicit reason for none |\n24\t| Approval and critic | Final scoped approval with date, approver and every frame ID/hash; independent critic rounds, reference work, row scores and PASS/FAIL; no open P0/P1 |\n25\t| Tool evidence | Runtime/tool versions, exact commands, stdout/exit, machine JSON and screenshots; declared test-only evidence excluded from actual approval |\n26\t\n27\tTokens include the desktop reference width and documented layout breakpoints needed for the authorized scope. They do not invent mobile/tablet styling while those phases are deferred. Breakpoint CSS variables are a handoff representation; standard CSS custom properties cannot be substituted directly into media-query conditions. The engineer translates those values into the project's existing build conventions.\n28\t\n29\t## JSON fields\n30\t\n31\t`tools/handoff-contract.mjs` is the executable validator. Use its field names exactly; do not infer an unvalidated schema from prose. The following is its authoring guide:\n32\t\n33\t- `version`, `phase`, `brief.path`, `rules.path`, and `settings` identify the contract and inputs. Settings provides `path`, `repository`, `stack` and `roles.engineer`, `roles.qaGate`, `roles.writer`, `roles.approver`, matching the actual project settings. Fannan copies those values; it never selects a stack. `settings.desktopWidths` lists the project's declared desktop CSS widths: unique positive integers that always include the canonical 1440 (for example `[1440]` or `[1440, 1280]`); a missing, duplicate or 1440-less list fails the contract in both phases (the wireframe is a desktop handoff too; there is no default list). It declares widths to check; it does not authorize a mobile phase.\n34\t- `pointOfView.idea` and `pointOfView.briefRefs` establish the visual thesis. A separate decision/section ledger links every section, grid break, token and expressive element to source lines and the relevant audience task.\n35\t- `languages` lists each `{lang,dir}`. Every screen has its page, `headingOutline`, `seoIntent`, `schemaIntent`, `images` with alt text/intent, and `actions` with standalone labels. Empty image/action arrays must match the real page; omission is not evidence of absence.\n36\t- `screens[].states` accounts for the seven state names **per language**, with `lang` and `dir`. Applicable states have `page` and `frame:{path,width:1440,version,sha256}`. A non-applicable state has `applicable:false`, `reason`, `reviewer` and `evidence`; default is always applicable. A page-level state may show the relevant component in that state; do not invent a global error screen for a static page.\n37\t- `screens[].components[].states` also accounts for each state, including justified N/A. Attach appearance, behavior, entry/exit triggers, keyboard behavior and recovery in the component specification referenced by the inventory. Every non-default component state must be represented by a real reference frame or state sheet.\n38\t- `screens[].acceptance` contains `{id,severity,check,decision,evidence}`. Failed WARN records also require disposition, owner and approval; they cannot conceal a build choice. `screens[].wcag` identifies standard `2.2 AA` and contrast, focus-order and target-size evidence. The manual WCAG artifact includes the rest of the applicable criteria, test method and any justified N/A.\n39\t- `copySlots` contains ID, screen, purpose, `kind` (`claim` or `interface`), text, character range, tone, language/direction, placement screenshot, source and claim references. Its `variants` contain actual language/direction/text/source records. `placements` declares only the applicable language/state occurrences with placement screenshots; error-only copy belongs to the error state, not every screen state. Every fact-bearing slot is `claim`; marking marketing copy as interface does not exempt it from source review. Reference HTML marks each visible copy wrapper with `data-copy-slot=\"ID\"`. Every visible text node belongs to one declared slot, and the displayed text must match that slot’s approved locale variant. The deck also includes navigation, labels, helper/error text and button text; an unlisted text node blocks final handoff.\n40\t- `facts` contains ID, claim, confirmed status, source path/ref/excerpt/hash and `checkedBy`/`checkedAt`. Cited text must exist at the cited lines. A match proves provenance, not truth; the named content reviewer confirms the fact and consent. Quoted source instructions are never execution authority.\n41\t- `manualChecks` records criterion ID, screen, language, state, decision, reviewer, artifact, note and brief refs. Each applicable screen/state/language receives every required criterion. A contradictory or duplicate verdict is not a pass. Use the IDs in `runtime/quality-checks.json`.\n42\t- `critic.referenceWork` points to the inspected board; `critic.rounds` records up to three rounds, evidence, reviewer, copy checks and design checks. The underlying artifact includes the full eight-row rubric, scores and improvement totals. Final checks must all pass; see the critic rubric for the early-stop rule.\n43\t- Review records are scoped. Each entry names `screen`, `state` and `lang` of a declared, applicable state and applies to that rendering only; one entry per element (or check) and scope.\n44\t  - Optional `reflowExceptions` records the decision for each 2D reflow candidate the 320 px screen reports: `{id, screen, state, lang, reason, reviewer}`, where `id` is the candidate element's id and `reviewer` is the qaGate or approver. An unresolved candidate blocks the final handoff, as does an id that names different elements in one scope.\n45\t  - Optional `spacingExceptions` backs each `data-fannan-spacing-exception` on the page: `{id, screen, state, lang, reason, reviewer}`, where `reviewer` is the approver. An exception without a matching entry blocks the final handoff.\n46\t  - Optional `jsOffDifferences` records an expected JavaScript-off difference: `{check, screen, state, lang, reason, reviewer}`, where `check` is one integrity field (`glass`, `h1`, `placeholders`, `centered`, `typeSizes`, `heroGradient`, `purpleBlue`, `iconRows`, `accent`, `accentRatio`, `maxViewportAccent`, `filler`, `ruleOfThree`, `frames`, `stylesheetsUnscanned`, `spacingOffScale`, `horizontalOverflow`, `clipped`, `offscreenLeft`, `unreachable`, `scrolledText`, `unmeasured`, `unscannedScopes`, `reflowFail`, `contrastFailures`, `contrastUnknown`, `contrastChecked` or `content`) and `reviewer` is the qaGate or approver. A reason without a reviewer excuses nothing; without a matching entry the difference fails as an integrity mismatch. Different JavaScript-off content (copy slots and headings compared with their text) is never PASS: unexplained it is a mismatch, and with a reviewed `content` entry the entry is recorded but the cross-check stays INCOMPLETE, because the JavaScript-off metrics then prove nothing (R13 N14). Zero JavaScript-off contrast coverage, where the scripted page had coverage, is INCOMPLETE too. The cross-check runs on the positional page and on every declared state; the main gates still fail scripted glass and the other hard fails.\n47\t- `designLink`, `tokens`, `assets`, `motion`, `toolEvidence`, `approval`, `openQuestions` and `placeholders` complete the package. Each of `tokens.json`, `tokens.css`, `tokens.buildScript`, `tokens.config` and `tokens.lock` has `{path,sha256}`. `tokens.buildEvidence.path` points to the `.build.json` receipt written by the reviewed Style Dictionary build, binding input, output, script, config and lock hashes. The checker never executes an arbitrary project-supplied script. Approval names its record, decision, approver and `frameIds` in the form `screen/lang/state/version/sha256`; the dated approval artifact repeats those exact IDs.\n48\t\n49\tCharacter counts are not permission to shorten approved content. For a multilingual slot use language-specific ranges when necessary, revise the contract and revalidate. Rendering, grapheme clusters and bidirectional order require visual review even if a string-length check passes.\n50\t\n51\t## Evidence and responsibilities\n52\t\n53\tThe writer owns exact copy, fact confirmation and wording review. Fannan owns design decisions and the package. The critic reviews independently. The approver owns the human decision. The project's engineer confirms that the package leaves no build question and implements only in the repository/stack from settings. The QA gate reuses the same criterion IDs against the implementation and records preview URL, commit SHA, build output, screenshots, test steps and actual result. Fannan's prototype evidence cannot stand in for that implementation QA.\n54\t\n55\tThe generated checklist is a review surface, not an authorization token. Compare its source JSON hash and artifact versions before relying on it. A full PASS requires all checks from the current machine gate plus verified manual artifacts and actual approval. A manually typed `PASS`, a copied report or a valid hash of fabricated evidence is insufficient; inspect provenance and authorship during review.\n56\t\n57\t## Missing inputs and change control\n58\t\n59\tAt draft phases, keep missing text visibly marked and register its owner. At Step 9, even one unresolved question or placeholder blocks. Missing licences, references, claims, tool evidence, role mappings or approval are treated the same way. The issue list states the exact item, owner, next action and impact. The task remains Stuck/On hold; there is no partial handoff labelled complete.\n60\t\n61\tAfter final approval, a change to source copy, tokens, state behavior, frame or acceptance requirement invalidates affected checks and approval. Create a fresh version and output directory; never overwrite evidence already cited by approval. Reuse unaffected evidence only when its dependency hashes and scope still match.\n62\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8f80f814-38ed-4ed2-80fc-18e1bfbe6740","uuid":"80c59ea3-bf5a-4112-a886-ec5fa0e65cf1","timestamp":"2026-10-18T03:25:45.194Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/panel-r14/fannan/release/references/handoff-contract.md","content":"# Step 9 package contract\n\n`handoff.json` is the authored source of truth. `HANDOFF.md` is its readable checklist, generated by the checker. Keep one set of criterion IDs; do not maintain a second design/QA acceptance list. Freeze the source JSON, reference frames and all linked evidence by version/hash. Any affected change requires fresh checks and approval. Paths in the JSON are resolved from the command's working directory; use absolute paths for release evidence to avoid ambiguity.\n\nThis contract specifies what each real project must supply. The included synthetic fixture is only an executable checker example. It is not a real project's copy, factual proof, approval, licensed asset set or completed handoff.\n\n## Fixed package contents\n\n| Artifact | Required contents and exact acceptance |\n|---|---|\n| `handoff.json` and generated `HANDOFF.md` | Version 1 record; project/phase; no open questions; no placeholders; all screens, locales and states; one criterion ID per check; actual evidence and owner/reviewer |\n| Source snapshot | Project settings, rules, brief, fact register and copy-deck revisions; source hashes and cited lines; current step and permitted scope |\n| Private design link | `designLink.url`, `private:true` and evidence of access/privacy; tool and frame IDs/version; no public upload as a shortcut |\n| `DESIGN.md` | Reviewed narrative and token frontmatter; Google format lint with zero errors; token decisions traced to brief lines |\n| Tokens | DTCG-style JSON containing type, spacing, colors and breakpoints; generated CSS variables; executable build script, actual config and locked dependency versions; no hand-edited generated CSS |\n| Screen/state inventory | Stable screen and component IDs; default, hover, focus, disabled, empty, loading and error all accounted for; N/A requires reason, reviewer and evidence; applicable states specify appearance, behavior and recovery |\n| Reference frames | 1440-pixel-wide PNGs; `screen--state--locale--vNN.png`; actual file hash and version; every applicable state/language; reference implementation HTML when used |\n| Final copy deck | Per-slot ID, screen, purpose, kind, exact final text, min/max character range, tone, language/direction, source lines, claim IDs and placement screenshot; variants use actual approved translations |\n| Assets | File, source URL/document, creator, licence/permission, consent record where needed, intended use and crop; font family/weight files and licence; file size and project's budget result |\n| Motion | Job, duration in ms, easing, trigger, affected element, interruption/cleanup, static reduced-motion fallback, no-JS and script-failure behavior |\n| Acceptance | Per-screen criteria with ID, BLOCK/WARN, observable pass/fail check, decision and evidence. Failed BLOCK stops. WARN needs impact, owner and approved disposition with no unresolved question |\n| Accessibility | WCAG 2.2 AA, contrast-pair table, focus order and captures, target sizes/exceptions, alt text and intent, heading outline, standalone action labels, manual audit and automated incomplete-result resolution |\n| Audit intent | One H1 per page; complete semantic outline; intent for every image's alt text; independent link/button labels; page purpose, search intent, proposed title/description, index/canonical decision, schema type and source-backed properties or an explicit reason for none |\n| Approval and critic | Final scoped approval with date, approver and every frame ID/hash; independent critic rounds, reference work, row scores and PASS/FAIL; no open P0/P1 |\n| Tool evidence | Runtime/tool versions, exact commands, stdout/exit, machine JSON and screenshots; declared test-only evidence excluded from actual approval |\n\nTokens include the desktop reference width and documented layout breakpoints needed for the authorized scope. They do not invent mobile/tablet styling while those phases are deferred. Breakpoint CSS variables are a handoff representation; standard CSS custom properties cannot be substituted directly into media-query conditions. The engineer translates those values into the project's existing build conventions.\n\n## JSON fields\n\n`tools/handoff-contract.mjs` is the executable validator. Use its field names exactly; do not infer an unvalidated schema from prose. The following is its authoring guide:\n\n- `version`, `phase`, `brief.path`, `rules.path`, and `settings` identify the contract and inputs. Settings provides `path`, `repository`, `stack` and `roles.engineer`, `roles.qaGate`, `roles.writer`, `roles.approver`, matching the actual project settings. Fannan copies those values; it never selects a stack. `settings.desktopWidths` lists the project's declared desktop CSS widths: unique positive integers that always include the canonical 1440 (for example `[1440]` or `[1440, 1280]`); a missing, duplicate or 1440-less list fails the contract in both phases (the wireframe is a desktop handoff too; there is no default list). It declares widths to check; it does not authorize a mobile phase.\n- `pointOfView.idea` and `pointOfView.briefRefs` establish the visual thesis. A separate decision/section ledger links every section, grid break, token and expressive element to source lines and the relevant audience task.\n- `languages` lists each `{lang,dir}`. Every screen has its page, `headingOutline`, `seoIntent`, `schemaIntent`, `images` with alt text/intent, and `actions` with standalone labels. Empty image/action arrays must match the real page; omission is not evidence of absence.\n- `screens[].states` accounts for the seven state names **per language**, with `lang` and `dir`. Applicable states have `page` and `frame:{path,width:1440,version,sha256}`. A non-applicable state has `applicable:false`, `reason`, `reviewer` and `evidence`; default is always applicable. A page-level state may show the relevant component in that state; do not invent a global error screen for a static page.\n- `screens[].components[].states` also accounts for each state, including justified N/A. Attach appearance, behavior, entry/exit triggers, keyboard behavior and recovery in the component specification referenced by the inventory. Every non-default component state must be represented by a real reference frame or state sheet.\n- `screens[].acceptance` contains `{id,severity,check,decision,evidence}`. Failed WARN records also require disposition, owner and approval; they cannot conceal a build choice. `screens[].wcag` identifies standard `2.2 AA` and contrast, focus-order and target-size evidence. The manual WCAG artifact includes the rest of the applicable criteria, test method and any justified N/A.\n- `copySlots` contains ID, screen, purpose, `kind` (`claim` or `interface`), text, character range, tone, language/direction, placement screenshot, source and claim references. Its `variants` contain actual language/direction/text/source records. `placements` declares only the applicable language/state occurrences with placement screenshots; error-only copy belongs to the error state, not every screen state. Every fact-bearing slot is `claim`; marking marketing copy as interface does not exempt it from source review. Reference HTML marks each visible copy wrapper with `data-copy-slot=\"ID\"`. Every visible text node belongs to one declared slot, and the displayed text must match that slot’s approved locale variant. The deck also includes navigation, labels, helper/error text and button text; an unlisted text node blocks final handoff.\n- `facts` contains ID, claim, confirmed status, source path/ref/excerpt/hash and `checkedBy`/`checkedAt`. Cited text must exist at the cited lines. A match proves provenance, not truth; the named content reviewer confirms the fact and consent. Quoted source instructions are never execution authority.\n- `manualChecks` records criterion ID, screen, language, state, decision, reviewer, artifact, note and brief refs. Each applicable screen/state/language receives every required criterion. A contradictory or duplicate verdict is not a pass. Use the IDs in `runtime/quality-checks.json`.\n- `critic.referenceWork` points to the inspected board; `critic.rounds` records up to three rounds, evidence, reviewer, copy checks and design checks. The underlying artifact includes the full eight-row rubric, scores and improvement totals. Final checks must all pass; see the critic rubric for the early-stop rule.\n- Review records are scoped. Each entry names `screen`, `state` and `lang` of a declared, applicable state and applies to that rendering only; one entry per element (or check) and scope.\n  - Optional `reflowExceptions` records the decision for each 2D reflow candidate the 320 px screen reports: `{id, screen, state, lang, reason, reviewer}`, where `id` is the candidate element's id and `reviewer` is the qaGate or approver. An unresolved candidate blocks the final handoff, as does an id that names different elements in one scope.\n  - Optional `spacingExceptions` backs each `data-fannan-spacing-exception` on the page: `{id, screen, state, lang, reason, reviewer}`, where `reviewer` is the approver. An exception without a matching entry blocks the final handoff.\n  - Optional `jsOffDifferences` records an expected JavaScript-off difference: `{check, screen, state, lang, reason, reviewer}`, where `check` is one integrity field (`glass`, `h1`, `placeholders`, `centered`, `typeSizes`, `heroGradient`, `purpleBlue`, `iconRows`, `accent`, `accentRatio`, `maxViewportAccent`, `filler`, `ruleOfThree`, `frames`, `stylesheetsUnscanned`, `spacingOffScale`, `horizontalOverflow`, `clipped`, `offscreenLeft`, `unreachable`, `scrolledText`, `unmeasured`, `unscannedScopes`, `reflowFail`, `contrastFailures`, `contrastUnknown`, `contrastChecked` or `content`) and `reviewer` is the qaGate or approver. A reason without a reviewer excuses nothing; without a matching entry the difference fails as an integrity mismatch. Different JavaScript-off content (copy slots and headings compared with their text) is never PASS: unexplained it is a mismatch, and with a reviewed `content` entry the entry is recorded but the cross-check stays INCOMPLETE, because the JavaScript-off metrics then prove nothing (R13 N14). Zero JavaScript-off contrast coverage, where the scripted page had coverage, is INCOMPLETE too. The cross-check runs on the positional page and on every declared state; the main gates still fail scripted glass and the other hard fails.\n- `designLink`, `tokens`, `assets`, `motion`, `toolEvidence`, `approval`, `openQuestions` and `placeholders` complete the package. Each of `tokens.json`, `tokens.css`, `tokens.buildScript`, `tokens.config` and `tokens.lock` has `{path,sha256}`. `tokens.buildEvidence.path` points to the `.build.json` receipt written by the reviewed Style Dictionary build, binding input, output, script, config and lock hashes. The checker never executes an arbitrary project-supplied script. Approval names its record, decision, approver and `frameIds` in the form `screen/lang/state/version/sha256`; the dated approval artifact repeats those exact IDs.\n\nCharacter counts are not permission to shorten approved content. For a multilingual slot use language-specific ranges when necessary, revise the contract and revalidate. Rendering, grapheme clusters and bidirectional order require visual review even if a string-length check passes.\n\n## Evidence and responsibilities\n\nThe writer owns exact copy, fact confirmation and wording review. Fannan owns design decisions and the package. The critic reviews independently. The approver owns the human decision. The project's engineer confirms that the package leaves no build question and implements only in the repository/stack from settings. The QA gate reuses the same criterion IDs against the implementation and records preview URL, commit SHA, build output, screenshots, test steps and actual result. Fannan's prototype evidence cannot stand in for that implementation QA.\n\nThe generated checklist is a review surface, not an authorization token. Compare its source JSON hash and artifact versions before relying on it. A full PASS requires all checks from the current machine gate plus verified manual artifacts and actual approval. A manually typed `PASS`, a copied report or a valid hash of fabricated evidence is insufficient; inspect provenance and authorship during review.\n\n## Missing inputs and change control\n\nAt draft phases, keep missing text visibly marked and register its owner. At Step 9, even one unresolved question or placeholder blocks. Missing licences, references, claims, tool evidence, role mappings or approval are treated the same way. The issue list states the exact item, owner, next action and impact. The task remains Stuck/On hold; there is no partial handoff labelled complete.\n\nAfter final approval, a change to source copy, tokens, state behavior, frame or acceptance requirement invalidates affected checks and approval. Create a fresh version and output directory; never overwrite evidence already cited by approval. Reuse unaffected evidence only when its dependency hashes and scope still match.\n","numLines":62,"startLine":1,"totalLines":62}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_bdrk_015HCFQ4as9RDCMbxZtnBH21","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
