{"type":"assistant","message":{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"I want to quickly check the git diff for regressions around N05, N06, N14, and N10, and look for potential gaps in Part A—specifically whether contrastProbe and motionFor snap are querying across shadow roots correctly or just the light DOM.\n\nI'm also thinking about `document.getAnimations()` and whether it captures shadow-including descendants per spec—likely yes for CSS animations/transitions, but JS-driven movement like setInterval inside an open shadow root wouldn't be caught by element snapshots. Since the threat model treats open shadow roots as ordinary patterns that must be fully detected, a setInterval-driven carousel inside a shadow root could slip past the snapshot and rAF counter, making this a plausible P2 gap worth flagging.\n\nI'm also weighing whether a 5-second auto-advancing carousel ignoring reduced motion is really an undetected honest mistake rather than obfuscation—since it's a common pattern (WCAG 2.2.2 territory) that the gate fails open on, I'll flag it as P2 with a caveat that manual review should catch it. For the designProbe glass and sheetGlass checks on styles/stylesheets, those seem fine as they already account for shadow scopes and wrap stylesheet access in error handling.\n\nNow I'm tracing a trickier edge case: pages loaded via file:// URLs might bypass Playwright's route interception (which mainly targets http/https), meaning linked stylesheets load directly from disk instead of being captured as \"served.\" Combined with Chrome's security restrictions treating file:// stylesheets as cross-origin, cssRules access would throw, leaving every linked sheet both unreadable and unserved—a gap worth flagging.\n\nI'm also checking that recursive @import handling correctly refuses data: URLs by checking href, and confirming that designProbe only inspects stylesheets from the main document and open shadow roots, not cross-origin frames, since those fail independently anyway.\n\nFor visibility checks, I'm verifying `shown` handles `collapse` and that opacity-0 text doesn't need special handling since it's already excluded. I'm also checking that `display:contents` elements with child spans still get measured correctly, and considering how overflow propagation behaves when `html` has visible overflow but `body` sets its own overflow rules.\n\nI'm then tracing combinations of `overflow-x` on `html` and `body` — both hidden, only one hidden, or `clip` instead of `hidden` — to confirm `clipBox` and `scrollerOf` correctly identify which element acts as the real clipping boundary and that offscreen content gets flagged as clipped in each case.\n\nI also confirm that `documentElement.scrollWidth` still reflects overflowed content width even when overflow is hidden, so the horizontal overflow check remains accurate. Then I verify the stepped-target logic for N15, confirm CDP's `DOM.getDocument` with pierce covers closed shadow roots and iframe documents for N16, note N14 is tested, and move into checking N10's package setup.\n\nChecking other filenames too — none trip the credential-word filter. I'm also noting that contrastProbe isn't composed, so contrastChecked misses shadow text, a P3 diagnostic issue. The design-quality doc also seems stale relative to R13's contrast handling, and separately omits mention of `portal` — minor doc drift, both P3.\n\nI should check local-fixture.mjs for a possible frames-negative regression. Then for N18, motionFor relies on isolatedPage(main) while snap only queries the light DOM, missing shadow content — I'm trying to recall whether document.getAnimations() per spec actually covers shadow-including descendants, since Chrome's implementation seems to include shadow tree animations.\n\nI'm uncertain whether this counts as a verified gap, so I'll mark it UNVERIFIED and note that JS-driven style changes on shadow elements via setInterval wouldn't get snapshotted either, giving a realistic example like a web